--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertCalled` and `assertNeverCalled` refuse an ordinary object, both with the same `LogicError`, because only a double keeps a record — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The boundary the erased parameter leaves open. What these two really take is
// a *double*, which is a class no program can name and therefore no registry
// row can write down; the declared parameter is `object`, so every instance
// satisfies it where the call is written and the narrower question is asked
// here, at run time.
//
// It is a `LogicError` rather than a failed assertion on purpose: a test that
// asserts about a real object's calls has not found a bug in the subject, it
// has asked a question that has no answer.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool;
}

final class RealMailer implements Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool {
        return true;
    }
}

Mailer $real = new RealMailer();
echo $real->send("ada@example.test") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";

// 1. Asking whether a real object was called.
try {
    Core\Test::assertCalled($real, Mailer::send);
} catch (LogicError $asked) {
    echo $asked->message, "\n";
}

// 2. The other member refuses the same way. The two agree because they read
// the same record through the same reader, which is what makes the message
// worth pinning: a second reader would drift from this one.
try {
    Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($real, Mailer::send);
} catch (LogicError $asked) {
    echo $asked->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
sent
Core\Test::assertCalled(): `RealMailer` is an ordinary object, and the record this reads is kept only by a double `Core\Test::double` or `Core\Test::partial` built
Core\Test::assertNeverCalled(): `RealMailer` is an ordinary object, and the record this reads is kept only by a double `Core\Test::double` or `Core\Test::partial` built
